INSERT INTO arya_knowledge (tenant_id, domain, topic, content, tags, source_type, source_title, status, language) VALUES

('varah', 'stories', 'The Cosmic Story: Creation as Narrative',
'The oldest Indian texts are not theology — they are stories. The cosmos begins with a question: what was there before everything? The answer is a story. The primordial waters, the first sound, the breath that became the world. Creation is not a fact — it is a narrative with a protagonist who wonders, acts, and is surprised by what emerges. This shapes all Indian storytelling: the universe itself is a story still being told. Every human story is a small universe. The teller is always inside the story, never outside it. The listener is always a participant. The fire at the centre is consciousness — everything else circles it.',
ARRAY['creation','cosmos','consciousness','origin'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Philosophy as Story: The Dialogue Tradition',
'Indian philosophical wisdom is almost never given as doctrine. It arrives as conversation — a student comes with a burning question, a teacher answers not with an answer but with a story, an image, a question back. The greatest truths are revealed in dialogue: between a teacher and student in a forest, between a king and a sage, between a warrior and his charioteer at the edge of a battlefield. The form itself is the teaching: truth cannot be stated, only encountered. The Socratic question, the indirect answer, the story that opens a door without pushing you through — these are the instruments. Every character in every story is both asking the question and containing the answer.',
ARRAY['philosophy','dialogue','teaching','wisdom'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Character Archetypes: The Eight Eternal Figures',
'Indian storytelling carries eight character types that recur across every tradition, region, and era.

THE RELUCTANT HERO: enormous capability, profound doubt. Goes to war carrying the weight of every person he must hurt. His paralysis is not weakness — it is full moral awareness.

THE LOYAL BETRAYED: extraordinary gifts undone by others'' smallness and his own absolute devotion. Does his duty to those who never deserved it. The tragedy is that he was right all along.

THE SILENT SUFFERER: endures what would break anyone, never loses herself, never asks to be saved, chooses her own moment to speak. Her silence is strategy and dignity.

THE WISE COUNSELLOR: sees everything, says only what can be heard, never acts from self-interest. Carries the burden of knowing more than he is permitted to say.

THE DHARMA-BETRAYER: abandons his own people for what he believes is a higher right. Traitor or the only one who truly understood? The story never decides.

THE DEVOTED SERVANT: asks nothing for himself, capable of anything in service of love. His devotion is not subservience — it is a form of freedom.

THE GIFTED DESTROYER: everything he touches becomes magnificent and then ruins itself. A hero who cannot be saved from his own gifts.

THE RETURNING KING: goes away, is tested beyond what any human should survive, comes back changed. The kingdom is never the same after he returns, and neither is he.',
ARRAY['archetype','character','hero','tragedy','dharma'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'The World''s Greatest Character Web: Complex Moral Drama',
'The greatest epic ever written is not about a war — it is about a family. What happens when cousins are raised together, love each other, and are placed in a situation where they must destroy each other to survive. Everyone in this story is right. Everyone is also wrong. The grandfather who could stop everything but does not. The blind king who sees more clearly than those with sight. The queen-mother who makes a choice at a riverbank that echoes for three generations. The teacher who trains the boys who will fight each other, and then must choose a side. The charioteer who is actually a god, and who gives one warrior the truth he needs to act — knowing it will cost everything. No character is simply good or evil. Everyone carries the weight of their own righteousness. That is why the story has never ended: because the moral problem it describes is still unsolved.',
ARRAY['epic','moral','family','war','character','duty'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Character Arcs No Writer Has Matched: The Perfect Human Drama',
'The story of the perfect man — and what being perfect costs. He is a king who gives up a kingdom without complaint. A husband who crosses an ocean. A son who obeys his father even when his father is wrong. His perfection is the source of his greatest suffering: because he never chooses himself, he loses what he loves most. The woman he loves is not a passive figure — she is the moral centre. She walks into fire not to prove herself to others but to prove something to herself. When she finally speaks, she says what she has been saying with her entire life. The villain of this story is consumed by beauty and desire, by the impossibility of possessing what is not his — the cautionary tale about the man who mistakes longing for love. Every character in this story is precisely the right amount of human.',
ARRAY['character','devotion','duty','sacrifice','love','dignity'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'The Panchatantra Principle: Story as the Highest Teaching',
'Three princes could not be taught through direct instruction. So a teacher told them stories — stories starring animals, because animals say the true thing plainly. The crow who used stones to raise the water level. The lion destroyed by flattery. The mouse who gnawed through the net that trapped the elephant. Each story contains a principle. The principle is never stated — you arrive at it yourself, which is why you cannot forget it. The story-within-a-story is the defining structure: every story contains another story, and that inner story contains the real teaching. A merchant tells a story to a thief to prevent his own murder. The frame is the context; the inner story is the content; the gap between them is where wisdom lives. Short, complete, specific, decisive. A story should leave no room for confusion about what happened — and total room for reflection about what it means.',
ARRAY['fable','wisdom','teaching','animal','structure','wit','frame'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'The Jataka Structure: One Life Moment, One Eternal Truth',
'Five hundred and forty-seven stories of lives and lessons. In each, a being is born — sometimes human, sometimes animal, sometimes a tree — and in that life, one moment arrives that tests everything. The doctor who could not save the patient but could hold his hand. The bird who gave its feathers to keep others warm and then flew anyway. The merchant who returned money that was not his, though no one would have known. Each story is short. Each story is complete. Each ends not with a moral but with a resonance — the feeling of having understood something that cannot be said directly. Goodness is practiced over lifetimes, not achieved in a single act. Every ordinary moment is a test. Every ordinary person is being formed by their choices. The protagonist always could have chosen wrong and chose right — not because it was easy, but because choosing right had become who they were.',
ARRAY['life','lessons','compassion','choice','moral','ordinary'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Poetry That Becomes Visual: The Art of Longing',
'The greatest classical Sanskrit poet wrote about seasons, about longing, about the moment just before reunion and the moment just after loss. His most famous poem is a message sent through a cloud: a man exiled from his wife asks a monsoon cloud to carry his words across a continent. The cloud becomes the reader; the landscape becomes the map of the man''s longing. His plays contain the most famous recognition scene in any literature: a king who forgot his wife, a ring that triggers memory, a woman who was almost a ghost. The technique is always the same: maximum restraint creates maximum emotion. The image is specific — not "flowers" but "a jasmine flower on the cheek of a woman sleeping." Not "grief" but "the way smoke lingers on clothes long after the fire is out." The visual image carries the emotional weight. The eye and the heart arrive at the same place.',
ARRAY['poetry','longing','imagery','love','loss','visual','seasons'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'The Modern Indian Soul: The Small Moment That Holds Everything',
'A writer from Bengal learned to see himself in stories that noticed small things: the way light fell on a widow''s hands, a child''s refusal to come indoors for supper, a man sitting by a river who had nothing left to lose and was, for that reason, entirely free. His stories do not build to climaxes — they build to recognitions, moments when something invisible becomes visible, and you realise it was always there. The poor do not merely suffer in his stories — they live, with full interior lives, with moments of beauty and pettiness and grace. The lonely do not merely long — they have specific, unrepeatable ways of being lonely. His women are rarely victims; they are navigators in landscapes built against them. The form is sparse; the emotion is full. Nothing is wasted. The last line is always the thing that was always the thing.',
ARRAY['modern','light','interior','woman','poverty','recognition','bengal'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'The Voice of Ordinary India: Dignity Without Glamour',
'The voice of ordinary India — the farmer too proud to beg and too poor to survive, the woman who holds her household together while the men collapse, the child who learns to read in secret and discovers that reading changes everything and nothing. These stories are uncomfortable. They do not offer easy resolutions. The farmer sells his ox and still cannot pay his debt. The woman who has been faithful all her life is abandoned. The child who should be the hero does not save the day. But there is always dignity — in the way a character refuses to be less than herself despite every pressure. In the way a hand reaches across a social boundary without announcement. In the way someone poor chooses to be generous when no one is looking. The lesson is never stated. The story shows you the world as it is, and leaves you alone with what you feel.',
ARRAY['farmer','poverty','dignity','woman','ordinary','india','social'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Kerala''s Silence: What Is Not Said Is the Story',
'Kerala''s greatest storyteller learned from the monsoon — the long silences between rain, the way mist holds a hillside, the moment when the river knows it is about to flood. His stories are about what people cannot say to each other: fathers and sons who have loved each other all their lives and never spoken it, women who carry knowledge they will never reveal, men who understand too late what they had. The technique is the omission: what is left out of a scene carries more weight than what is included. A character leaves a room; the gaze stays on the object left behind. A conversation ends; the silence that follows is the real exchange. His retelling of the great epic from the second brother''s point of view is proof that every story has a protagonist who has been waiting for someone to notice him.',
ARRAY['silence','kerala','father','son','unsaid','monsoon','malayalam'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Where the Mythic and the Real Become One Body',
'His first novel is set in a place that may not exist. The landscape is Kerala, the time is unclear, the events are both completely ordinary and completely impossible. A man walks across a mountaintop. He carries his whole life with him. The mountain knows he is there. This is the central technique: the mythic and the mundane are not separate realms that occasionally intersect — they are the same thing, seen from two different angles. His humour is dark and precise: the bureaucrat who is also a demon, the revolutionary who is also a coward, the saint who is also a thief. The absurdity is not comic — it is tragic. The tragedy is not heavy — it is strangely light, the way the sky after rain is lighter than the sky before. His prose is compressed to the point where every sentence contains a paragraph.',
ARRAY['myth','absurd','dark','kerala','landscape','compressed','malayalam'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Love, Madness, and Humanity in One Sentence',
'He went to prison. He went to war. He was hungry for years. And then he wrote stories in which love is the most natural thing in the world, in which the insane are often the only sane people, in which a wall between two houses becomes the site of a complete and sufficient romance. His language is simple because he has gone past the need for complexity. His characters are broken because he had been broken and knew that broken things can still be whole. He wrote about women who chose themselves before that was a concept. He wrote about the poor with such warmth that poverty became, in his hands, not a condition but a way of seeing the world more clearly than the rich. His stories end before they are finished, because life does too.',
ARRAY['love','madness','simple','humanity','wall','poor','freedom','kerala'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Navarasa: Nine Emotional Essences That Power Every Story',
'Every story draws its energy from one of nine essences. These are not genres — they are fundamental human experiences that stories transmit.

SHRINGARA: love, beauty, the fullness of the heart when it has found what it was looking for. Also the ache of longing. Use when the story is about connection, the miracle of being seen.

KARUNA: compassion, grief, the weight of loss or unfulfilled hope. The feeling after a hard day, after loss, after a shift that took too much.

VIRA: courage, heroism, the moment when a small person rises to meet something enormous. Use before something difficult, for stories of beginning.

SHANTA: peace, serenity, the settling of everything into its right place. The rasa of bedtime, of completion, of quiet understanding. Use for rest and resolution.

ADBHUTA: wonder, amazement, the world showing its depth. Use for children, curious minds, people who have forgotten to be surprised.

HASYA: joy, lightness, truth arrived at from an unexpected angle. Use for light moments, for recovery, for when the world needs to feel manageable.

BIBHATSA: the righteous feeling that something is deeply wrong and must change. Use for moral stories, for when someone needs their anger confirmed.

RAUDRA: righteous anger, the protective fire. Use when someone needs strength and permission to act.

BHAYANAKA: the facing of fear, the courage that lives inside darkness. Use for anxiety, for approaching something terrifying, for the night before.',
ARRAY['navarasa','emotion','rasa','love','compassion','courage','joy','fear','wonder'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Natyashastra: The Five-Stage Story Arc',
'Two thousand years before the modern screenplay manual, a precise structure for satisfying stories was described. Every complete story moves through five stages.

ARAMBHA (Beginning): The seed is planted. A character wants something, or something disrupts their world. The reader understands what kind of story this is going to be.

YATNA (Effort): The character moves toward what they want. Obstacles appear. The effort reveals who the character truly is.

PRAPTYASHA (Glimmer of Hope): Something suggests the goal is reachable. This is the turn that makes the story believable.

NIYATAPTI (Near-Achievement): The goal is almost reached. Maximum tension — it could go either way.

PHALAGAMA (The Result): Not always the result the character wanted. Sometimes the result is understanding rather than achievement. The story is complete when the character has been fully transformed by what they went through.

The interval — the pause in the middle — is where the audience absorbs what has happened and prepares for what is coming. Silence and pause are structural elements, not gaps.',
ARRAY['structure','arc','five stages','screenplay','drama','plot','natyashastra'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en'),

('varah', 'stories', 'Regional Voices: The Many Mouths of India''s Story Tradition',
'India''s story tradition is not one river but a delta. Each region has its own mouth.

KERALA: Stories of rain and silence. Humid, interior, the voice of what cannot be said directly. The landscape itself carries emotional weight: the backwaters, the paddy fields in October, the sound of a noon bell from a distant temple. Stories often end in understanding, not resolution.

BENGAL: Stories of light and earth. Light through windows, light on water, light at the edge of things. Also the heaviness of the earth — the village that floods, the soil that takes everything you give it. Emotions are specific, quiet, devastating.

RAJASTHAN: Stories of desert wisdom. The camel who knows where water is. The woman who keeps a whole village''s history in her embroidery. The star that guides the merchant home after twenty years. Wisdom is practical and earned.

TAMIL SANGAM: Emotional precision. The oldest love poetry in the world named the landscape of feeling — mountains for longing, forests for patient waiting, seashores for separation. Every emotion has its geography.

UP AND BIHAR: Stories of ordinary dignity. The man who is despised and is nevertheless the most human person in the room. The woman who runs the household while everyone else performs importance.',
ARRAY['regional','kerala','bengal','rajasthan','tamil','folk','landscape','voice'], 'tradition', 'India Story Tradition', 'published', 'en')

